{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_018eADDiLfcxrfTQJpQq6eFz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! The C library consists of a single translation unit (`src/lib.c`) exposing\n4\t//! exactly one public symbol, `read_side_info` (see `include/lib.h`). There are\n5\t//! no namespace/renaming macros in the public header, so the linker symbol name\n6\t//! is identical to the source-level name.\n7\t//!\n8\t//! Behaviour is reproduced exactly, including the quirks of the original code:\n9\t//!   * `get_bits` advances `bs->pos` *before* the limit check and still returns\n10\t//!     0 (without reading) when the limit is exceeded.\n11\t//!   * `get_bits` reads bytes via raw pointer arithmetic and may read past the\n12\t//!     end of the caller's buffer, just like the C.\n13\t//!   * `region_count[2]` is deliberately left untouched on the short-block\n14\t//!     path (the C never assigns it there), so the caller's previous value\n15\t//!     survives.\n16\t//!   * All arithmetic wraps/truncates exactly as the C integer conversions do.\n17\t\n18\t#![allow(non_camel_case_types)]\n19\t\n20\tuse std::ffi::c_int;\n21\t\n22\t/// Mirrors the C `bs_t`:\n23\t///\n24\t/// ```c\n25\t/// typedef struct {\n26\t///     const uint8_t *buf;\n27\t///     int pos, limit;\n28\t/// } bs_t;\n29\t/// ```\n30\t#[repr(C)]\n31\tpub struct bs_t {\n32\t    pub buf: *const u8,\n33\t    pub pos: c_int,\n34\t    pub limit: c_int,\n35\t}\n36\t\n37\t/// Mirrors the C `L3_gr_info_t`:\n38\t///\n39\t/// ```c\n40\t/// typedef struct {\n41\t///     const uint8_t *sfbtab;\n42\t///     uint16_t part_23_length, big_values, scalefac_compress;\n43\t///     uint8_t global_gain, block_type, mixed_block_flag, n_long_sfb, n_short_sfb;\n44\t///     uint8_t table_select[3], region_count[3], subblock_gain[3];\n45\t///     uint8_t preflag, scalefac_scale, count1_table, scfsi;\n46\t/// } L3_gr_info_t;\n47\t/// ```\n48\t#[repr(C)]\n49\tpub struct L3_gr_info_t {\n50\t    pub sfbtab: *const u8,\n51\t    pub part_23_length: u16,\n52\t    pub big_values: u16,\n53\t    pub scalefac_compress: u16,\n54\t    pub global_gain: u8,\n55\t    pub block_type: u8,\n56\t    pub mixed_block_flag: u8,\n57\t    pub n_long_sfb: u8,\n58\t    pub n_short_sfb: u8,\n59\t    pub table_select: [u8; 3],\n60\t    pub region_count: [u8; 3],\n61\t    pub subblock_gain: [u8; 3],\n62\t    pub preflag: u8,\n63\t    pub scalefac_scale: u8,\n64\t    pub count1_table: u8,\n65\t    pub scfsi: u8,\n66\t}\n67\t\n68\t/// The three scalefactor-band tables that the C declares as function-local\n69\t/// `static const uint8_t` arrays.\n70\t///\n71\t/// `sr_idx` is derived from the header and can reach 8 (sample-rate index 3\n72\t/// combined with the MPEG1 / not-MPEG2.5 version bits), which indexes one row\n73\t/// *past* the end of every `[8][...]` table. The C therefore performs an\n74\t/// out-of-bounds read whose result depends purely on how the compiler happened\n75\t/// to lay the tables out in `.rodata` — it is not stable even between two\n76\t/// builds of the C itself (gcc `-O2` reverses the order relative to gcc `-O0`).\n77\t///\n78\t/// The reference build (`c_src/CMakeLists.txt`) sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, so\n79\t/// it compiles at `-O0`, where gcc — and clang at any level — emits the tables\n80\t/// in declaration order:\n81\t///\n82\t/// ```text\n83\t///   g_scf_long   +0     184 bytes\n84\t///   (padding)    +184     8 bytes of zeros\n85\t///   g_scf_short  +192   320 bytes\n86\t///   g_scf_mixed  +512   320 bytes   (.rodata ends at +832)\n87\t/// ```\n88\t///\n89\t/// This `#[repr(C)]` blob reproduces that layout byte for byte (all fields have\n90\t/// alignment 1, so the offsets above are exact). Consequently row 8 of `long`\n91\t/// yields the 8 zero pad bytes followed by the start of `short`'s row 0, and\n92\t/// row 8 of `short` aliases `mixed`'s row 0 — matching the C exactly. Row 8 of\n93\t/// `mixed`